<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adam is the name given in Genesis 1–5 to the first human, a figure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnosticism">Gnosticism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the Baháʼí Faith. In Genesis 2 God forms Adam out of the dust of the ground, places him in the Garden of Eden and forms a woman, Eve, as his companion; after they eat from the tree of knowledge, God condemns Adam to labour on the earth. According to Christianity, this sin introduced death and sin into the world. In Islam, Adam is considered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa">Khalifa</a> on earth, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> tells that he was forgiven by God when he repented.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adam.jpg" alt="Adam" width="330" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michelangelo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michelangelo,_Creation_of_Adam_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
